From: Fabien Danieau <fabien.danieau@pollen-robotics.com>
Subject: [PATCH] webrtcsink: add mpph264enc encoder support

Add support for Rockchip's MPP hardware H.264 encoder, so that webrtcsink's
congestion control can move its bitrate. Without it webrtcsink logs "Bitrate
handling is not supported yet for mpph264enc" at every session and the link
never adapts, which on wifi means dropped frames instead of lower quality.

Modelled on `webrtcsink: add v4l2h264enc encoder support`: configure_encoder,
is_bitrate_supported, and the bitrate getter and setter. The bitrate property
is `bps`, in bits per second; MPP derives bps-min and bps-max from it when they
are left at zero. `gop` is raised from its default of one keyframe per second,
since webrtcsink requests keyframes via PLI.

The converter chain is deliberately left at the default videoconvert !
videoscale. An earlier version of this patch replaced it with an identity, on
the belief that it cost a CPU pass per frame; measured on the board, both
elements run in passthrough and reuse the input buffer when the camera's format
and size already suit the encoder. The identity also broke the DOWNSCALED
mitigation mode: below 1 Mbps webrtcsink asks for 360p, and with nothing to
scale the caps cannot be met.

Level handling is absent for the same reason as for v4l2h264enc: the encoder
knows the negotiated resolution and frame rate and calculates its own level,
which mpph264enc gained in https://github.com/JeffyCN/mirrors/pull/83.

Upstream-Status: Pending [https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/gstreamer/gst-plugins-rs/-/issues/826]

--- a/net/webrtc/src/webrtcsink/imp.rs
+++ b/net/webrtc/src/webrtcsink/imp.rs
@@ -816,6 +816,14 @@ fn configure_encoder(enc: &gst::Element, start_bitrate: u32) {
                 enc.set_property("key-int-max", 256u32);
                 enc.set_property_from_str("rate-control", "cbr");
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => {
+                // `bps` is the target; MPP derives bps-min/bps-max from it when they
+                // are left at 0. `gop` is in frames and -1 means "one per second",
+                // which would put a keyframe on the wire every second for no reason:
+                // webrtcsink asks for one via PLI when a consumer actually needs it.
+                enc.set_property("bps", start_bitrate);
+                enc.set_property("gop", 2560i32);
+            }
             "nvv4l2h264enc" => {
                 enc.set_property("bitrate", start_bitrate);
                 if enc.has_property("preset-level") {
@@ -1170,6 +1178,7 @@ impl VideoEncoder {
                 | "vaapivp8enc"
                 | "vaapivp9enc"
                 | "qsvh264enc"
+                | "mpph264enc"
                 | "nvv4l2h264enc"
                 | "nvv4l2vp8enc"
                 | "nvv4l2vp9enc"
@@ -1201,6 +1210,7 @@ impl VideoEncoder {
             | "vah265enc" | "vah265lpenc" | "vaav1enc" | "vaav1lpenc" => {
                 (self.element.property::<u32>("bitrate") * 1000) as i32
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => self.element.property::<u32>("bps") as i32,
             "openh264enc" | "nvv4l2h264enc" | "nvv4l2vp8enc" | "nvv4l2vp9enc" | "rav1enc"
             | "nvv4l2av1enc" => (self.element.property::<u32>("bitrate")) as i32,
             _ => return Err(WebRTCSinkError::BitrateNotSupported),
@@ -1244,6 +1254,7 @@ impl VideoEncoder {
             "openh264enc" | "nvv4l2h264enc" | "nvv4l2vp8enc" | "nvv4l2vp9enc" | "nvv4l2av1enc" => {
                 self.element.set_property("bitrate", bitrate as u32)
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => self.element.set_property("bps", bitrate as u32),
             "rav1enc" => self.element.set_property("bitrate", bitrate),
             _ => return Err(WebRTCSinkError::BitrateNotSupported),
         }
